gate label means reported in earlier evaluation metrics). search_eval_evidence_selected.json and paper_data.json contain no search-plane label means. claims_grounding.json last claim has final.* source_ids only.\n  - requested_fix: In the abstract and the Table 4 caption, say that the prior constants are hand-set values that the configuration notes describe as aggregate label means from earlier evaluation metrics, and that these means are not retained in the evidence. Drop or soften the unqualified 'search-plane' wording elsewhere, and do not present the grounded claim as backed by the final-evaluation label metrics.\n- `grounding-source-mismatch-1` (Method (Experimental protocol, Compared predictors); Supplementary Information A and B): The claims_grounding.json entries for these facts cite only final.qualification.status (a 'completed' flag) and result.best_correct_score (a score). Neither id carries any protocol constant, dataset fact or implementation detail. The facts are in source_evidence files that no grounding source id references, so the evidence trail does not resolve the visible wording. The text does cite these files, and I checked those cited files against the manuscript statements and found them consistent.\n  - claim: Protocol facts (36 episodes, 720 labeled instances, 8 reruns, 20 derangements, 200 bootstrap replicates, d=10/10/20) and diagnostic constants (8 folds, 4 partitions, 1000 draws, weights, shrinkage 0.6/0.4, margin 0.02), plus the hash-disjointness of final and search data.\n  - evidence: claims_grounding.json claims 'Protocol facts...' and 'Diagnostic constants...' list final.qualification.status and result.best_correct_score as their source_ids. paper_data.json grounding_sources holds value-only entries for these ids. The constants appear in source_evidence/evaluator_final.py (lines 66-72, 761-812), task_contract.json, final_resources_manifest.json and selected_config_program.py (lines 35-46).\n  - requested_fix: Anchor each protocol, dataset-disjointness and constant statement in the manuscript to the specific source_evidence file and line, as the text mostly already does. State that these items are read from source files rather than from the sealed metrics, and drop any implication that final.qualification.status or the search score supports them.\n# Draft reference gap analysis\n\n**Status:** ready. The draft is well cited on Shapley estimation, value-function semantics, stability measures and tabular data/models. All 26 `\\citep` keys resolve in `references.bib`. Quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json` and need no citations. Three narrow gaps justify a targeted retrieval pass.\n\n## Missing literatures\n- Run-to-run stability and per-instance uncertainty of sampling-based local explainers (LIME/SHAP).\n- Delete-a-group jackknife variance estimation, and shrinkage/empirical-Bayes estimation.\n- Use of feature attributions in deployed tabular decision settings.\n\n## Rewrite targets\n\n### 1. related-work-instability-comparators (high, comparator) — Related Work\n- **Anchor:** \"Local explanations are known to vary under perturbation and between methods ... Our label isolates a different and narrower source of variation\".\n- **Why:** The paper's core claim is a per-run reproducibility estimate for a stochastic explainer. The related work cites only generic robustness and disagreement papers, plus population-level uncertainty (Williamson2020Efficient). It does not cite work that measures repeated-run instability or per-instance sampling uncertainty of LIME/SHAP-style estimators. These are the closest comparators, and the novelty positioning is thin without them.\n- **Search topics:** stability indices and repeatability of LIME under random sampling; confidence intervals or standard errors for Kernel SHAP and sampled Shapley estimates; reliability of SHAP across repeated runs and budgets.\n\n### 2. method-jackknife-shrinkage-rationale (medium, background) — Method\n- **Anchor:** \"delete-one-group jackknife covariance\"; \"shrink the simulated value toward a budget-specific prior\".\n- **Why:** The diagnostic's two statistical ingredients have no cited source. Both topics were in the original query plan but did not reach the bibliography. One or two foundational references would ground the terminology without changing the story.\n- **Search topics:** delete-a-group jackknife variance estimation; empirical Bayes shrinkage toward a prior.\n\n### 3. intro-deployment-claim (low, positioning) — Introduction\n- **Anchor:** \"Shapley-value attributions are a standard way to justify an individual prediction ... in credit, medical and similar settings\".\n- **Why:** The deployment-practice claim (\"run the explainer once and report what it returns\") is supported only by the original SHAP paper.\n- **Search topics:** explainable ML in deployment, and how practitioners use feature attributions.\n\n## Deliberately not flagged\n- Dataset citations (German Credit, Give Me Some Credit, Pima). The datasets are described with sources in the appendix, and extra citations would only add bibliography size.\n- Shapley estimation variants and value-function semantics, which are already well covered.\n- Deep tabular models, which are explicitly out of scope.\nfigures:\nerror_accounting.pdf\nerror_accounting.pdf.prov.json\nerror_accounting.png\nerror_accounting.png.prov.json\nmain_comparison.pdf\nmain_comparison.pdf.prov.json\nmain_comparison.png\nmain_comparison.png.prov.json\nprotocol_overview.png\nprotocol_overview.png.prov.json\nprotocol_overview.prompts.json\n\nscientific-evidence:\nbundle.manifest.json\nledger.jsonl\nschemas\nspec.md\nverification-report.json","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
